{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "07-template.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "The check in the template",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Model the check the page runs, find the precondition it depends on and what breaks without it, and prove the whole path, from the role to what the page shows.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The browser gets the compressed set, and the directive asks it questions:\n\n```ts\n/** What the frontend asks of the set it was sent. */\nconst can = (perms: Permissions, t: string, r: string, a: string) =>\n  [`${t}-${r}:${a}`, `${t}-${r}:*`, `${t}-*:${a}`, `${t}-*:*`]\n    .some((k) => perms[k] === true);\n\n// *ifPermissions=\"doc; tenants: [tenant1]; actions: [Create]; match: any\"\nfunction ifPermissions(perms: Permissions, resource: string,\n    tenants: string[], actions: string[], match: \"any\" | \"all\"): boolean {\n  const pairs = tenants.flatMap((t) => actions.map((a) => [t, a] as const));\n  const allowed = ([t, a]: readonly [string, string]) =>\n    can(perms, t, resource, a);\n  return match === \"all\" ? pairs.every(allowed) : pairs.some(allowed);\n}\n```",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`can` looks for any of the four keys that could apply, and takes a `true` among them as a yes. In the model, that is: some key that applies says yes.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- The frontend's can: some key that applies to the request says yes. -/\ndef canWire (p : Perms T R A) (t : T) (r : R) (a : A) : Bool :=\n  p.any fun e => e.1.covers t r a && e.2\n\ntheorem canWire_grants {p : Perms T R A} (hp : grantsOnly p) (t : T) (r : R) (a : A) :\n    canWire p t r a = allows p t r a := by\n  rw [allows_grants hp]\n  apply Bool.eq_iff_iff.mpr\n  simp only [canWire, List.any_eq_true, Bool.and_eq_true]\n  constructor\n  · rintro ⟨e, he, hc, -⟩; exact ⟨e, he, hc⟩\n  · rintro ⟨e, he, hc⟩; exact ⟨e, he, hc, hp e he⟩",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "On a set of grants, `can` is right. On anything else:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "example : canWire blocked .t1 .doc .delete = true ∧ allows blocked .t1 .doc .delete = false := by decide",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> Hand `can` a role straight from the server, with its deny, and it shows tenant1 a Delete button the role forbids. `can` is correct only on sets of grants, and nothing says so: compression's output and a role have the same type, `Permissions`. A precondition that lives only in the head of the person who wrote the function is a bug waiting for the next person.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The model can carry the precondition in the type. A `Wire` is a set together with a proof that it is a set of grants, and the only way to make one from a role is to compress it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- What goes over the wire: a set of grants, with the proof. -/\ndef Wire (T R A : Type) := { p : Perms T R A // grantsOnly p }\n\ndef toWire (p : Perms T R A) (ts : List T) (rs : List R) (as : List A) : Wire T R A :=\n  ⟨compress p ts rs as, compress_grants p ts rs as⟩\n\ntheorem canWire_toWire (p : Perms T R A) (ts : List T) (rs : List R) (as : List A)\n    (t : T) (r : R) (a : A) (ht : t ∈ ts) (hr : r ∈ rs) (ha : a ∈ as) :\n    canWire (toWire p ts rs as).val t r a = allows p t r a := by\n  rw [canWire_grants (toWire p ts rs as).property, toWire, allows_compress p ts rs as t r a ht hr ha]",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`{ p : Perms T R A // grantsOnly p }` is a **subtype**: values of `Perms` that come with a proof of `grantsOnly`; `.val` is the set and `.property` the proof. TypeScript cannot carry the proof, but it can carry the name: a branded type, `type WirePermissions = Permissions & { readonly __wire: true }`, returned only by `compress` and the only type `can` accepts, makes the mistake above a type error.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Some and every",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "inductive Match where\n  | any | all\n  deriving Repr, DecidableEq\n\n/-- *ifPermissions=\"r; tenants: ts; actions: as; match: m\": some pair, or every pair, of a tenant and\n    an action is allowed on r. -/\ndef ifPermissions (p : Perms T R A) (r : R) (ts : List T) (as : List A) : Match → Bool\n  | .any => ts.any fun t => as.any fun a => allows p t r a\n  | .all => ts.all fun t => as.all fun a => allows p t r a\n\n#eval ifPermissions editor .doc [.t1] [.create, .read] .all\n#eval ifPermissions editor .user [.t1] [.read, .delete] .any\n#eval ifPermissions editor .user [.t1] [.read, .delete] .all",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!note] Another reading of \"all\"\n> The code takes `all` over every pair of a tenant and an action. \"Every listed tenant may do some listed action\" is another reasonable reading, and someone writing a template may assume it. The model had to pick one, the code's, and the docs should say it in a sentence.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> What does `*ifPermissions=\"doc; tenants: []; actions: [Delete]; match: all\"` show, to a user with no permissions at all?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "example : ifPermissions ([] : Perms Tenant Res Act) .doc [] [.delete] .all = true := rfl",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> `[].every(f)` is `true`: every member of an empty list passes, since none fails. So a template whose tenant list comes out empty (the user's tenants not loaded yet, a filter that matched none) shows a Delete button to everyone. It is the empty case of \"for all\", and [Logic and proof technique](#course=logic&lesson=4) has it as a law: a `∀` over nothing is true. Here it is a hole.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The fix asks `all` for at least one tenant and one action:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- The check with all asking for at least one tenant and one action. -/\ndef ifPermissions' (p : Perms T R A) (r : R) (ts : List T) (as : List A) : Match → Bool\n  | .any => ifPermissions p r ts as .any\n  | .all => !ts.isEmpty && !as.isEmpty && ifPermissions p r ts as .all\n\nexample : ifPermissions' ([] : Perms Tenant Res Act) .doc [] [.delete] .all = false := rfl",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "End to end",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The theorem that ties it together: what the page decides from the compressed set is what the role says, for any tenants and actions of the universe, either way of matching.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem ifPermissions_compress (p : Perms T R A) (uts : List T) (urs : List R) (uas : List A)\n    (r : R) (ts : List T) (as : List A) (m : Match)\n    (hr : r ∈ urs) (hts : ∀ t ∈ ts, t ∈ uts) (has : ∀ a ∈ as, a ∈ uas) :\n    ifPermissions (compress p uts urs uas) r ts as m = ifPermissions p r ts as m := by\n  have same : ∀ t ∈ ts, ∀ a ∈ as, allows (compress p uts urs uas) t r a = allows p t r a :=\n    fun t ht a ha => allows_compress p uts urs uas t r a (hts t ht) hr (has a ha)\n  apply Bool.eq_iff_iff.mpr\n  cases m with\n  | any =>\n    simp only [ifPermissions, List.any_eq_true]\n    constructor\n    · rintro ⟨t, ht, a, ha, h⟩; exact ⟨t, ht, a, ha, by rw [← same t ht a ha]; exact h⟩\n    · rintro ⟨t, ht, a, ha, h⟩; exact ⟨t, ht, a, ha, by rw [same t ht a ha]; exact h⟩\n  | all =>\n    simp only [ifPermissions, List.all_eq_true]\n    constructor\n    · intro h t ht a ha; rw [← same t ht a ha]; exact h t ht a ha\n    · intro h t ht a ha; rw [same t ht a ha]; exact h t ht a ha",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "With `allows_expand` for the server's step, it is the whole path: role, expanded, compressed, sent, checked, each step proved to keep what the role allows, for every role, inside the universe.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem any_of_all' (p : Perms T R A) (r : R) (ts : List T) (as : List A) (h : ifPermissions' p r ts as .all = true) : ifPermissions' p r ts as .any = true := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "With the fix, `all` implies `any`, as anyone reading a template expects. Prove it. (Without the fix it is false: the empty list.)",
      "leanSolution": "  simp only [ifPermissions', ifPermissions, Bool.and_eq_true, Bool.not_eq_true', List.isEmpty_eq_false_iff,\n    List.all_eq_true, List.any_eq_true] at *\n  obtain ⟨⟨hts, has⟩, h⟩ := h\n  obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := List.exists_mem_of_ne_nil ts hts\n  obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := List.exists_mem_of_ne_nil as has\n  exact ⟨t, ht, a, ha, h t ht a ha⟩",
      "hints": [
        "Unfold both sides into statements about members: `simp only [ifPermissions', ifPermissions, Bool.and_eq_true, Bool.not_eq_true', List.isEmpty_eq_false_iff, List.all_eq_true, List.any_eq_true] at *`.",
        "The hypothesis now says `ts ≠ []`, `as ≠ []`, and every pair is allowed. `List.exists_mem_of_ne_nil` gives a member of a non-empty list.",
        "With `t ∈ ts` and `a ∈ as` in hand, the goal is `∃ t, t ∈ ts ∧ ∃ a, a ∈ as ∧ …`: `exact ⟨t, ht, a, ha, h t ht a ha⟩`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem not_any_of_all : ¬ ∀ p : Perms Tenant Res Act, ifPermissions p .doc [] [.delete] .all = true → ifPermissions p .doc [] [.delete] .any = true := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "And without the fix, `all` does not imply `any`. Prove it, with the empty set as the counterexample.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro h\n  have := h [] rfl\n  revert this\n  decide",
      "hints": [
        "As in lesson 3: `intro h`, use `h` on a set, and the result is a false statement Lean can compute.",
        "`have := h [] rfl`, then `revert this` and `decide`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> The page's check is right only on sets of grants, a precondition the code's types did not carry; the model carries it in a subtype, and TypeScript can carry its name in a branded type. `all` over an empty list is true, so an empty tenant list showed everything; asking for at least one fixes it, and then `all` implies `any`. End to end, what the page shows is what the role allows, for every role, inside the universe.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "One thing is left: the expand loop itself. The capstone models it step for step and holds it to the specification.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
